::error file=.env,line=2,col=11,title=EG014 invalid-value::LOG_LEVEL in .env has the invalid value verbose: expected one of debug, info, warn or error%0A%0AWhy: The app expects LOG_LEVEL to be one of debug, info, warn or error; an invalid value usually crashes it at startup or, worse, is silently misread.%0A%0AFix: Set LOG_LEVEL to one of debug, info, warn or error in .env:2. If the schema is wrong, update LOG_LEVEL at .envguard.yaml:7.
::warning file=.env,line=5,col=1,title=EG005 empty-required-value::SESSION_SECRET is empty in .env but required by .env.example%0A%0AWhy: An empty SESSION_SECRET is usually read as "set" and passes presence checks, then fails later (empty URLs, empty credentials, zero ports).%0A%0AFix: Set a value for SESSION_SECRET in .env:5. If an empty value is intentional, mark the template line optional: `SESSION_SECRET= # optional` in .env.example:6.
::warning file=.env,line=8,col=1,title=EG002 undocumented-variable::FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT is set in .env but not documented in any template%0A%0AWhy: Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from the templates; without an entry, FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT will be missing wherever this file is not copied.%0A%0AFix: Add `FEATURE_NEW_CHECKOUT=` (with an empty or placeholder value, never the real one) to .env.example, or remove it from .env if nothing uses it.
::warning file=.env,line=9,col=1,title=EG002 undocumented-variable::CACHE_DIR is set in .env but not documented in any template%0A%0AWhy: Teammates, CI and deploy targets learn which variables exist from the templates; without an entry, CACHE_DIR will be missing wherever this file is not copied.%0A%0AFix: Add `CACHE_DIR=` (with an empty or placeholder value, never the real one) to .env.example, or remove it from .env if nothing uses it.
::warning file=.env,line=9,col=11,title=EG016 unresolved-reference::CACHE_DIR references APP_HOME, which is not defined in .env or the env files loaded with it%0A%0AWhy: Loaders that expand references substitute an empty string for an undefined APP_HOME, so CACHE_DIR silently ends up incomplete (for example a URL without a host).%0A%0AFix: Define `APP_HOME=` in .env (checked: .env and .env.example), give the reference a default such as `${APP_HOME:-value}`, or add APP_HOME to scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml if the process environment provides it.
::error file=.env.example,line=4,col=14,title=EG010 possible-secret-in-template::DATABASE_URL in .env.example looks like a URL with an embedded password%0A%0AWhy: A template is shared with everyone who can read the repository; a real credential there is leaked, and it stays in git history even after it is removed.%0A%0AFix: Replace the value with an empty one or a placeholder (`DATABASE_URL=` or `DATABASE_URL=<your-value>`). If it is a real credential, rotate it now: it has been exposed. If it is a deliberate fake, use an obvious placeholder or add `# envguard-ignore EG010` on the line above.
::notice file=.env.example,line=9,col=1,title=EG004 declared-but-unused::LEGACY_API_KEY is declared in .env.example but never read by the code, env files, Compose files or workflows%0A%0AWhy: Unused variables make templates longer and send people looking for values nothing needs; they are often left over after a refactor. Note: 1 dynamic lookup in this app (a variable name computed at run time) could not be analyzed and may read it.%0A%0AFix: If nothing needs LEGACY_API_KEY, remove it from .env.example:9 and from the real env files. If a tool outside the scanned code reads it (Prisma, Docker, a framework), add LEGACY_API_KEY to scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml.
::error file=.env.production,title=EG001 missing-variable::STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is declared in .env.production.example but missing from .env.production%0A%0AWhy: Anything that reads STRIPE_SECRET_KEY in the production environment gets no value, which usually fails only at runtime or silently falls back to a default.%0A%0AFix: Add `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=` to .env.production (it is declared in .env.production.example:2) and set its value. If it is not needed there, mark it optional in the template (`STRIPE_SECRET_KEY= # optional`) or put `# envguard-ignore EG001` on the line above it in .env.production.example.
::warning file=.env.production,line=4,col=11,title=EG015 unsafe-production-value::REDIS_URL points to a local address in production (.env.production)%0A%0AWhy: Inside a production container or server, localhost is the machine itself, not your database or API; this is almost always a development value that slipped through.%0A%0AFix: Set REDIS_URL in .env.production to the production host.
::error file=.env.production,line=6,col=9,title=EG014 invalid-value::WORKERS in .env.production has the invalid value 0: is below the minimum 1%0A%0AWhy: The app expects WORKERS to be an integer between 1 and 64; an invalid value usually crashes it at startup or, worse, is silently misread.%0A%0AFix: Set WORKERS to an integer between 1 and 64 in .env.production:6. If the schema is wrong, update WORKERS at .envguard.yaml:15.
::warning file=.env.production,line=8,col=1,title=EG006 duplicate-key::DEBUG is defined 2 times in .env.production (lines 7 and 8)%0A%0AWhy: Most loaders (dotenv, python-dotenv, Docker) silently use the last definition, so editing the first one has no effect and the file says two different things.%0A%0AFix: Keep one definition of DEBUG in .env.production: delete line 8 or the earlier one at line 7, keeping the value you intend.
::warning file=.env.production,line=8,col=7,title=EG015 unsafe-production-value::DEBUG is enabled in production (.env.production)%0A%0AWhy: debug mode in production exposes stack traces, settings and sometimes an interactive console to anyone who triggers an error.%0A%0AFix: Set `DEBUG=false` in .env.production.
::error file=.env.production,line=10,col=8,title=EG007 syntax-error::missing '=' after key%0A%0AWhy: Env loaders skip or misread malformed lines, so the affected variable silently ends up with a wrong value or none at all.%0A%0AFix: Write assignments as KEY=value. Keys cannot contain spaces, and a bare 'export KEY' without a value is not supported.
::warning file=.env.production.example,line=2,col=1,title=EG013 environment-drift::STRIPE_SECRET_KEY is declared for production but not for staging%0A%0AWhy: Environments that are supposed to run the same code should need the same variables; a variable documented for only some of them is often forgotten when the next deploy reaches the others.%0A%0AFix: Add `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=` to .env.staging.example, or move it to .env.example if every environment needs it. If it really applies only to production, add `# envguard-ignore EG013` above .env.production.example:2.
::warning file=.env.staging.example,line=2,col=1,title=EG013 environment-drift::STAGING_BANNER is declared for staging but not for production%0A%0AWhy: Environments that are supposed to run the same code should need the same variables; a variable documented for only some of them is often forgotten when the next deploy reaches the others.%0A%0AFix: Add `STAGING_BANNER=` to .env.production.example, or move it to .env.example if every environment needs it. If it really applies only to staging, add `# envguard-ignore EG013` above .env.staging.example:2.
::notice file=.github/workflows/deploy.yml,line=14,col=30,title=EG012 ci-secret-undocumented::workflows use secret FLY_API_TOKEN, but no template or schema documents it%0A%0AWhy: Whoever sets up a fork, a new environment or a new repository has to discover FLY_API_TOKEN by reading workflow files; a missing one usually shows up as a failed deploy.%0A%0AFix: Document it: add `FLY_API_TOKEN=` to .env.example with a comment such as `# CI: set in GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions (Secrets tab)`, or declare it under `variables:` in .envguard.yaml.
::notice file=.github/workflows/deploy.yml,line=15,col=30,title=EG012 ci-secret-undocumented::workflows use variable DEPLOY_REGION, but no template or schema documents it%0A%0AWhy: Whoever sets up a fork, a new environment or a new repository has to discover DEPLOY_REGION by reading workflow files; a missing one usually shows up as a failed deploy.%0A%0AFix: Document it: add `DEPLOY_REGION=` to .env.example with a comment such as `# CI: set in GitHub → Settings → Secrets and variables → Actions (Variables tab)`, or declare it under `variables:` in .envguard.yaml.
::warning file=docker-compose.yml,line=17,col=28,title=EG011 compose-undefined-variable::docker-compose.yml interpolates ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}, which is not defined in .env (the file Compose reads) or any template%0A%0AWhy: Compose substitutes an empty string for an unset POSTGRES_PASSWORD and only prints a warning, so the container starts with a broken value.%0A%0AFix: Add `POSTGRES_PASSWORD=` with a value to .env and document it in a template, or give it a default in the Compose file: `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:-value}`.
::error file=src/server.js,line=23,col=33,title=EG003 used-but-undeclared::ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY is read by the code but not declared in any env file, template or schema%0A%0AWhy: Nothing documents that ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY must be set, so a new environment or teammate will not provide it and the code reads an empty value at runtime.%0A%0AFix: Add `ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY=` to .env.example (read at src/server.js:23), then set it in each real env file. If the platform provides it, add ANALYTICS_WRITE_KEY to scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml.
::notice file=worker/main.py,line=6,col=30,title=EG003 used-but-undeclared::WORKER_CONCURRENCY is read by the code but not declared in any env file, template or schema%0A%0AWhy: The code falls back to a default, so nothing breaks if WORKER_CONCURRENCY is unset, but it is invisible to anyone configuring the app and the default may be wrong for production.%0A%0AFix: Document it by adding `WORKER_CONCURRENCY= # optional` to .env.example (read at worker/main.py:6). If you do not want it documented, add WORKER_CONCURRENCY to scanner.ignore_variables in .envguard.yaml.
EnvGuard: 6 errors, 10 warnings, 4 info in 10 files.
